  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "Suppose that \\(4^{x_1} = 5,\\) \\(5^{x_2} = 6,\\) \\(6^{x_3} = 7,\\) \\(\\ldots,\\) \\(127^{x_{124}} = 128.\\) What is \\(x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_{124}?\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(4\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": "7/2"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12b-2005-p13.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "From \\(4^{x_1} = 5\\) we get \\(x_1 = \\log_4 5,\\) and in general \\(x_k = \\log_{k+3}(k+4).\\)\n\nThe product telescopes: \\[ \\begin{aligned} &x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_{124} \\\\ &= \\log_4 5 \\cdot \\log_5 6 \\cdots \\log_{127} 128 \\\\ &= \\log_4 128. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nSince \\(128 = 2^7\\) and \\(4 = 2^2,\\) this equals \\(\\dfrac{7\\log 2}{2\\log 2} = \\dfrac72.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Each equation gives \\(x_k = \\log_{k+3}(k+4)\\)",
        "The product telescopes to \\(\\log_4 128\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
